  "worked_solution": "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,\u2295\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,\u2295\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks.",
